  "contents": "Is this clause enforceable in a residential lease in Ontario? Cite the section.\n\nRead the clause as a term of an ordinary private residential tenancy in Ontario that the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 covers: the rental unit has no exemption under section 6.1, and it is not student housing, a care home, a co-operative or a mobile home park.\n\nClause: The Tenant shall not allow any animal kept in the unit to damage the unit or to disturb other residents.\n\nEnd your answer with these two lines:\nVerdict: enforceable (or: Verdict: not enforceable)\nSection: <the number of the one section of the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 that decides it>\n",
  "config": {
   "max_output_tokens": 16000
  }
 },
 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:09:10.316993Z",
 "text": "Yes, this clause is enforceable. \n\nUnder **section 14** of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* (RTA), a provision in a tenancy agreement is void only if it prohibits the *presence* of animals in or about the residential complex. Because this clause permits the tenant to keep animals in the unit and merely requires the tenant not to allow them to damage the unit or disturb other residents—obligations that align with the Act's rules on tenant-caused damage and interference with reasonable enjoyment (though any enforcement or eviction through the Landlord and Tenant Board remains subject to the statutory thresholds in sections 34, 62, 64, and 76)—it is not rendered void by section 14.\n\nVerdict: enforceable\nSection: 14",
 "finish_reason": "STOP",
